    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "837beecf-4239-4b3d-b683-1d611af3da2e",
      "key": "6cb00c88-652c-4594-bacf-0961eddd954a",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureTitle",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "Itinerary"
        },
        {
          "alias": "richTextContent",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Ch3\u003EDay 1\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe board our private executive coach at our designated pick up points, and en route to our hotel we visit the English Heritage\u2019s Lullingstone Villa. One of the best Roman villa survivals in Britain, Lullingstone offers a tantalising glimpse into Roman domestic life over three centuries. This luxurious villa was begun in about AD 100 and developed by successive wealthy owners. It includes a heated bath suite and an underground pagan \u2018cult room\u2019, with a wall painting of water deities. We continue to the 4* Ashford International Hotel on the outskirts of Ashford for a five night stay on a half board basis.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 2\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOur Blue Badge guide meets us at our hotel this morning and accompanies us throughout the day. Our guided walking tour of the UNESCO listed city of Canterbury takes in the city walls and includes a visit to the Cathedral, dating back to 597AD when St Augustine, sent by Pope Gregory the Great as a missionary, established his seat in Canterbury. In 1170 Archbishop Thomas Becket was murdered in the Cathedral and ever since the Cathedral has attracted thousands of pilgrims, as told famously in Geoffrey Chaucer\u0026#39;s \u2018Canterbury Tales\u2019. This afternoon we visit the English Heritage\u2019s Dover Castle and Secret Wartime Tunnels, dating back to 1066 and one of the mightiest of fortresses in Western Europe. Guarding the Straits of Dover, the Castle occupies a site that has witnessed more than 2,000 years of history.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 3\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith our Blue Badge guide this morning we visit the National Trust\u2019s Sissinghurst Castle Garden. Developed around the surviving parts of an Elizabethan mansion, the gardens provide outstanding design and colour throughout the season. We continue to the National Trust\u2019s Smallhythe Place. Nestled among the rolling Kent countryside, the corridors of this early 16th century cottage resonate with the vibrant spirit of its theatrical former owner, Victorian actress Ellen Terry.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 4\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis morning we visit Charleston House, the home of the artists Duncan Grant and Vanessa Bell (sister of Virgina Woolf), that became the country meeting place for the group of writers, artists and intellectuals known as the Bloomsbury Group. We visit Great Dixter House and Gardens this afternoon with our Blue Badge guide, once the family home of the late Christopher Lloyd who devoted his lifetime to creating one of the most experimental, exciting and constantly changing gardens of our time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 5\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith our Blue Badge guide this morning we visit Hever Castle and Gardens. Dating back to 1270 when the gatehouse, outer walls and the inner moat were first built, the Tudor manor house was constructed within the walls 200 years later by the Boleyn family, and this was the childhood home of Anne Boleyn, Henry VIII\u2019s second wife. We continue to the National Trust\u2019s Ightham Mote, a superb moated manor house and garden, nestling in a sunken valley and dating from 1330. The main features of the house span many centuries and include the Great Hall and Tudor chapel with a painted ceiling.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 6\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter checking out of our hotel, and en route home we visit the National Trust\u2019s Chartwell property in Kent, the family home of Sir Winston Churchill from 1924 until the end of his life. The rooms and gardens remain much as they were when he lived there and contain personal memorabilia, books and family photographs. Taking carefully selected comfort stops en route we return back to our original pick up points.\u003C/p\u003E\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureDescription",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": ""
        },
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureSummary",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003ENB:- Please note we reserve the right to alter the above itinerary if required for operational\nreasons. Given the historic nature of the places visited, a certain degree of walking will be involved, and this tour may not be suitable for those with mobility problems.\u003C/p\u003E\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
